 are  concerns within the unionist community  in particular that need a comprehensive  response the way to do that isn't for  the british government to act  unilaterally  and to brief against the eu in the way  that they have this week encouraging the  prime minister to to act like he's acted  on ukraine in relation to um the  protocol  and urging him to rip it up  that kind of aggressive language does  nothing uh to move us forward on what is  a difficult issue you and i have spoken  about the fallout from brexit on the  island of ireland before that we've just  had an election in northern ireland  which was a difficult election it was a  polarizing election  and the challenge for us all is to  listen to all communities unionists and  nationalists and people who don't  describe themselves as either  and try to ensure that the agreements  that we've signed in the past which is  now international law  is applied with the flexibility and the  pragmatism to respond to all of those  concerns as opposed to  legislating unilaterally  for the concerns of one community in  northern ireland which it seems to me is  what the british government is now  saying that they will do as soon as this  week  if the eu doesn't give them everything  they want so that is not the way to  approach a problem with a neighbor with  a friend and with allies and partners  which is what the uk has right across  the european union right now  you saying that um  about boris johnson shouldn't take the  same approach with the protocol as he  did with ukraine what exactly did you  mean by that well what i mean by that is  that uh lord trust who of course was the  the former negotiator on brexit issues  who was uh one of the architects of the  northern ireland protocol and the  withdrawal agreement which allowed the  uk to leave the eu in a structured and  managed way and he did a good job in  many ways for the uk but he on the front  of the telegraph this week urging the  prime minister to show the leadership  that he's shown on ukraine and to rip up  the northern ireland protocol i mean  what we are getting there is a senior  figure in the conservative party calling  on his prime minister to break  international law and to create huge  problems uh for britain's neighbor here  in ireland  and of course unnecessary tension with  the eu  at a time when we all need to be working  together to solve problems not least of  course in ukraine uh there are almost 30  000 ukrainians have come to ireland so  far seeking refuge and safety from uh  from the war in ukraine five and a half  million  across the eu  and we have huge challenges to overcome  together the idea that we would ratchet  up the tension on something else an  issue that we've been dealing with now  for six years which is the decision of  the people of the united kingdom to  leave the european union and all that  flows from that in terms of disruption  and tension  uh that we would ratchet that up now to  to new heights is deeply unhelpful  particularly when the eu  wants to actually continue to negotiate  wants to show flexibility at once to  compromise to actually recognize that  there are legitimate concerns held  within the unionist community in  northern ireland that need a  comprehensive response the primary one  being that we need to treat goods that  are staying in northern ireland  differently to goods that are at risk of  traveling on through northern ireland  into the eu and the eu single market and  the eu is willing to do that and  dramatically change the number of checks  on those goods if they can find a  partner in london to do it with them so  liz trust the foreign secretary says the  current eu proposals failed to properly  address the real issues affecting  northern ireland and in some cases would  take us backwards and if you look at  what some of what the uk wants you know  as you're talking about there changing  the burden of proof for goods remaining  in northern ireland expanding the  trusted trader policy simplifying the  process so traders have to provide less  information perhaps action on grace  periods and vat  can you understand some of the  frustration in westminster  that they believe that the eu has been  heavy-handed in imposing some of these  checks in a very very delicate as you  know of course  border in the irish sea uh the dp  currently refusing uh to set up a power  sharing arrangement in northern ireland  so time really is of the essence can you  understand some of the frustration in  the in westminster at  what they would perceive as a reluctance  from the eu to act decisively or quickly  enough  so can i tell you i've been in these  discussions for more than five years now  uh i have been in negotiations with i  think four or five different brexit  ministers  on behalf of the british government and  all good people  um but let me tell you ireland is also  frustrated  we are dealing with the consequences now  of a decision by the british people on  our own country that's cost us hundreds  of millions of euros that is risking the  peace process and its institutions on  the island of ireland  and so you know when we focus on  frustrations  uh we need to think beyond westminster  yes of course liz trust and she's a good  person and i know her well uh she wants  to see changes in terms of how the  protocol is implemented  and she is perfectly entitled to seek  those changes she has a partner in mar  sefkovic who's the vice president of the  european commission who wants to do that  with her but there's no way the eu can  compromise if the if the uk is  threatening unilateral action to pass  domestic legislation to set aside  international obligations under an  international treaty that don't forget  the uk was the primary designer of  along with with the eu so yes i  understand the frustrations and in  particular i understand the frustrations  in the unionist community i've spoken to  many unionists who want to see  pragmatism and real flexibility so the  trade within the united kingdom in other  words trade between great britain and  northern ireland uh is not disrupted in  any way that is not absolutely necessary  and we can get there to a landing zone  if we work in partnership but you know  saber rattling and grandstanding um  in westminster ratcheting up tension is  not the way to do it  and what i am saying and i've invited uh  uh liz trust to to come to dublin to  talk to me in detail about those  concerns i've offered to come to london  in fact i've come to london many times  on these issues i think mars stephanie  is in the same position but look at a  time when the world needs the western  world to be united  to be acting in concert  um to solve problems together  this is a problem we need to solve  together the last thing ireland wants  the last thing the eu needs is tension  with a country the size and the  influence of the united kingdom so let's  work together through the summer get  these issues resolved get the  institutions back up and running in  northern ireland which is  the northern ireland one the final thing  i'd say is sophie that um you know there  is an impression often across the united  kingdom that northern ireland is deeply  unhappy with the protocol and that a  majority of people want to change that  is simply not true the majority of  people in northern ireland have just  voted for candidates that actually are  in favor of the protocol because of how  it manages the disruption of brexit 53  out of the 90 mlas that were elected in  northern ireland would vote in favor of  the protocol tomorrow if asked to do so  what they want  is pragmatism and flexibility in terms  of how it's implemented that's what the  eu wants to offer that's what dublin  wants to help facilitate and i hope  that's what the british government will  work with us on in the weeks ahead yeah  which is the point that i made to quasi  quoting about the majority on the  northern lined assembly 