tion because the protocol  isn't being implemented and the uk has  decided unilaterally to put in place  grace periods and so on but the eu in an  effort to try to build partnership set  aside that legal action they've frozen  it uh in an effort to try to negotiate a  sensible way forward and you know see  terrorism if  that's an indication of where the eu is  on this space  you know people need to look at that the  eu isn't threatening anything and i  think for us to focus on how the eu  would respond to the british government  deliberately breaching international law  is probably where we shouldn't be right  now instead we need to be trying to  avoid that situation but i think  everybody understands uh if the  uh if the british government decides to  set aside international law to create  significant uncertainty on the island of  ireland in terms of single market  membership and a whole range of other  things well then of course there's a  consequence the eu is forced to respond  but that is not where we want to be  where we want to be is good neighbors  good friends and solving problems  together and i would encourage the  influencers within the british  government to ensure that that's the  course that they decided the eu would be  forced to respond but i'm still just  unclear on what what that response would  be would it be yes i don't think you are  unclear actually but i i'm not here this  morning to talk about understanding um  uh the ratcheting up of tension i'm here  this morning to try to find solutions  and that's where i hope the british  government will be this week as well and  just very finally you know as you say  you know we have spoken  numerous times over the last few years  on brexit-related things it does strike  me this morning that you do seem  particularly frustrated i guess as a  word uh perhaps even even angry would  that be a fair summary of of where your  head is at right now well i mean i think  you know if you look at the comments  that i've made this week at the comments  that the t-shirt has made this week you  know we are both  friends of britain you know um  england and britain have shaped who i am  who many in the irish cabinet are you  know many of my family are uh are  british citizens um  some of my family work  and employ a lot of people across the uk  i've been to university in england there  isn't an anti-british bone in my body  quite the opposite but what i see at the  moment is a british government  making statements briefing against the  eu  and creating a lot of tension in my  country your closest neighbor and also  potentially  being on the verge of making a decision  that could fundamentally undermine the  functioning of the institutions of the  peace process in northern ireland let's  not forget this is not only about  unionism of course it needs to be partly  about union unionism but a majority of  people in northern ireland voted against  brexit and would vote against brexit  again in the morning if it was put to  them a majority of people in northern  ireland are in favor of the protocol  because they see that it does a  reasonable job to manage the disruption  of brexit in in the irish circumstances  there is a minority a large minority  within unionism who are unhappy with the  protocol there are solutions that we can  put in place that can ease that concern  and that's what we need to focus on  doing together  as opposed to the british government  acting on its own illegally in a way  that doesn't reflect majority opinion in  northern ireland and perhaps most  importantly sends a message to the world  that this british government when it  suits them will set aside international  law i don't say things like that lightly  i mean my job is to be a diplomat i'm a  foreign minister i'm a defense minister  and i want a really good relationship  with my closest neighbor  but what's happening at the moment has  forced ireland into taking a much more  strident position  and responding honestly uh to the  uh unhelpful briefings that we're  getting from very very senior levels  within the british government this week  which seems to be laying the groundwork  for a decision which i believe  could be deeply harmful for the  relationship between britain and ireland  uh if we don't see sense in the next few  days  okay simon coveney thank you very much  for being on the program this morning  simon comey

